 data provenance, rights to training and customer data, dependence on third-party models, evaluation evidence, inference cost behaviour and AI-specific governance and regulation."], ["Do you need access to the code?", "Read access to repositories, or a supervised session, gives far stronger evidence than documents and interviews alone. Where access is limited, the report says which findings rest on representations rather than inspection."], ["What does a risk-rated report contain?", "Each finding has a severity, the evidence behind it, its likely impact on value or the plan, and a recommended response such as a price adjustment, a condition, a warranty or a post-close action."]], "related": ["/technical-due-diligence/", "/insights/ai-startup-technical-due-diligence-checklist/", "/insights/iso-42001-vs-nist-ai-rmf/"], "citations": [{"title": "AI Risk Management Framework", "url": "https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework", "publisher": "NIST"}, {"title": "ISO/IEC 42001:2023 AI management systems", "url": "https://www.iso.org/standard/42001", "publisher": "ISO"}, {"title": "SP 800-218, Secure Software Development Framework (SSDF) Version 1.1", "url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/218/final", "publisher": "NIST", "date": "2022-02"}, {"title": "Datasheets for Datasets", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.09010", "publisher": "arXiv (Gebru et al.)"}, {"title": "Model Cards for Model Reporting", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03993", "publisher": "arXiv (Mitchell et al.)"}, {"title": "Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (Artificial Intelligence Act)", "url": "https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689/oj/eng", "publisher": "EUR-Lex", "date": "2024-06-13"}]}-->
<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>A private equity investor was preparing to invest in a software company whose growth story rested on AI: models that automated part of its customers' work and data assets that, the deck said, competitors could not replicate. The commercial and financial diligence was well under way. What the deal team lacked was an independent view of whether the technology, the data rights and the team matched the story, and what it would cost to fix anything that did not.</p>
<p>The timeline was set by the deal, so the review had to be structured, evidence-led and focused on findings that could change price, terms or the post-close plan.</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Evidence over assertion</strong>: every finding tied to something inspected, not only something said in an interview.</li>
<li><strong>Proprietary versus borrowed</strong> made explicit: which capabilities the company built, and which depend on third-party models or APIs.</li>
<li><strong>Data rights traced</strong> from source to model to product, including customer data used for training.</li>
<li><strong>Costs that scale</strong>: inference and cloud spend modelled against the growth plan.</li>
<li><strong>A report the deal team can use</strong>: risk-rated, with recommended responses, readable by non-engineers.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<p>The review was organised into workstreams, each with its own evidence requests and interview plan. AI governance was assessed against the functions of the NIST AI Risk Management Framework (govern, map, measure, manage)<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>, with ISO/IEC 42001 used as the reference for what a management system for AI looks like<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. The comparison is in <a href="/insights/iso-42001-vs-nist-ai-rmf/">ISO 42001 vs NIST AI RMF</a>.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Evidence</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Data room<em>documents, contracts</em></span><span>Repositories<em>read access</em></span><span>Cloud billing<em>cost exports</em></span><span>Interviews<em>leadership and engineers</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Platform</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Architecture review<em>scalability, resilience</em></span><span>Code and delivery<em>quality, practices</em></span><span>Security<em>SDLC and controls</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>AI</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Model provenance<em>built, tuned or bought</em></span><span class="hl">Data rights<em>source to product</em></span><span>Evaluation evidence<em>quality claims</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Economics and people</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Cost model<em>inference and cloud</em></span><span>Team and key people<em>concentration risk</em></span><span>Roadmap realism<em>plan vs capacity</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Output</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Risk register<em>severity and evidence</em></span><span>Deal implications<em>price, terms, conditions</em></span><span>100-day plan<em>post-close actions</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Review architecture: evidence feeds workstreams, workstreams feed one risk register, the register feeds the deal.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Request</b><span>Issue a targeted evidence list per workstream, prioritised by deal risk</span></li>
<li><b>Inspect</b><span>Review architecture, repositories, pipelines, billing and contracts</span></li>
<li><b>Interview</b><span>Test what was inspected with the people who built and run it</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Trace</b><span>Follow data and models from source through training to the product</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Rate</b><span>Score each finding for severity and likelihood; record the evidence behind it</span></li>
<li><b>Report</b><span>Summarise for the investment committee with recommended responses</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Every finding carries its evidence, so the deal team can see what was verified and what rests on representations.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Secure development practices were reviewed against the NIST Secure Software Development Framework, which groups practices into preparing the organisation, protecting the software, producing well-secured software and responding to vulnerabilities<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. The full checklist I use is in <a href="/insights/ai-startup-technical-due-diligence-checklist/">the AI startup technical due diligence checklist</a>, and supply chain questions in <a href="/insights/ai-model-supply-chain-security/">AI model supply chain security</a>.</p>

<h2>The hard part: proving data rights, not just data</h2>
<p>Showing that a company has data is easy. Showing that it may use that data for the purpose the plan depends on is not. The provenance workstream asked, for each dataset behind a model:</p>
<ul>
<li>Where it came from, under which contract or licence, and whether that permits training and commercial use.</li>
<li>Whether customer data was used to train shared models, and whether customer contracts allow it.</li>
<li>Whether the dataset is documented in a form like a datasheet, covering motivation, composition and collection<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</li>
<li>Whether models carry documentation of intended use and evaluation, in the spirit of model cards<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Regulation was mapped to where the company sells. For customers in the European Union, the review considered how the EU AI Act might classify the product and which provider or deployer obligations could apply<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>In AI diligence, the most valuable finding is often not a technical flaw but a missing right. A gap in data permissions can matter more to value than any code quality issue.</p></div>

<h2>How the engagement ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Deal team</small><ol><li>Set key questions</li><li>Arrange access</li><li class="hl">Decide price and terms</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Diligence lead</small><ol><li>Run workstreams</li><li class="hl">Rate findings</li><li>Present report</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Target company</small><ol><li>Provide evidence</li><li>Join interviews</li><li>Respond to findings</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Findings were shared with the target for factual accuracy before the final report.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Phase 1</small><b>Scope</b><ul><li>Deal thesis and key risks</li><li>Evidence request list</li><li>Access arrangements</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 2</small><b>Inspect</b><ul><li>Architecture and code</li><li>Billing and cost model</li><li>Contracts and data sources</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 3</small><b>Test</b><ul><li>Interviews</li><li>Provenance tracing</li><li>Draft findings</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 4</small><b>Report</b><ul><li>Risk-rated register</li><li>Committee briefing</li><li>Post-close plan</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Phased diligence: scope to the deal thesis first, then inspect, test and report.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Scope to the deal thesis</td><td>Exhaustive technical audit</td><td>Effort goes to what can change price or terms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Repository access</td><td>Documents and interviews only</td><td>Inspection gives stronger evidence than representation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Dedicated provenance workstream</td><td>Fold into legal review</td><td>Data rights need technical tracing, not only contract reading</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recognised frameworks as reference</td><td>Ad hoc checklist</td><td>Findings are comparable and defensible</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recommended response per finding</td><td>List of issues</td><td>Deal team can act: price, condition, warranty or plan</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: insist on repository and billing access early, and separate verified findings from representations in the report. Differently: start the data rights tracing on the first day, in parallel with legal counsel. It is the slowest workstream and the one most likely to change the deal.</p>
<p>If you're planning an investment, the <a href="/technical-due-diligence/">technical due diligence</a> page describes how I run these reviews, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
